---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="Slop Hill as flat gold and black shapes: a kitchen at the foot, two towers on the slope with a gap between them, small houses, a solid line straight up the ridge and a dashed line around the contour to the house at the top, two drones in the air"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    The first order has a shortest route, and it may not have the battery to come back: #07
    is on the hilltop, the ridge line is fastest, only the contour route returns. By the sixth
    order the deadline matters more than the distance. Five drones each find a good route and
    then wait at the same corridor and the same two charging pads. #13 lives on the far side
    of that corridor. #20 is hotpot for four, and only the heavy drone can carry it.
  </p>
  <p>
    For twelve weeks this course studies one kitchen's one evening, and turns a program that
    can find a route into a planner that can schedule twenty dinners. Every improvement has
    to answer three questions: which assumption changed, why the code changed the way it did,
    and what the evidence is.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Design and improve the algorithms, one constraint at a time. The problem grows in five
    steps: find a route (weeks 1–3); find a route the drone can actually fly and return from
    (week 4); order many deliveries for one drone (weeks 5–6); divide the work between drones
    that share two charging pads (weeks 7–8); make the plans of five drones executable
    together in one shared airspace (weeks 9–12). Each step starts from the method you
    already have, breaks it on a small example, and makes you change the state, the cost or
    the check that was wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every week you write a prediction before you run anything, derive one thing by hand,
    implement one key function, test it against a counterexample, and explain the result
    under the objective the course fixed. You leave with a cumulative planner — search,
    scheduling, space-time coordination — and the arguments, tests and records that say what
    it guarantees and what it does not.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Third-year undergraduates who have done introductory programming and data structures:
    functions, loops, lists and dictionaries, queues and priority queues, basic graphs, and
    enough algebra to sum costs along a path. No robotics, no flight control, no prior
    optimisation course. If you can write a loop that pops the smallest item from a queue,
    you can start; if you want a course about real drones and real airspace rules, this is
    not it — every number here is a teaching setting, and the policies page says so.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is assessed</h2>
  <p>
    Two individual assignments. Assignment 1 (40%, due Friday of week 7) is a single-drone
    planner: correct search, the full round trip with energy, and a justified order for six
    deliveries. Assignment 2 (60%, due Friday of week 12) extends it to five drones, two
    charging pads and the shared corridor, and has to show that route cost really feeds back
    into the schedule. Weekly tutorials are not graded; they are where the two assignments
    get built.
  </p>

  <h2>Three orders you will keep meeting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>#07, mapo tofu for the house on the hilltop.</strong> Far and urgent. The
      ridge route is fastest and burns the return energy; the contour route is slower and
      gets back.</li>
    <li><strong>#13, the house across the corridor.</strong> Its shortest route passes the
      one gap on the hill that only one drone can use at a time.</li>
    <li><strong>#20, hotpot for four.</strong> Only the heavy drone type can carry it, which
      decides who takes it before anything about efficiency is discussed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>Twelve Monday lectures, one question each, from a graph to a plan that flies.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>Twelve Wednesday tutorials: predict, derive, implement, test, explain.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>The two assignments, their dates, weights and marking criteria.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>The convenor and the tutor, and who to ask about what.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
